Richard Bos wrote:
>
> Quite apart from whether you can: you do not want to. You may think you
> do, but when you get a bit more experience, you will realise that you
> don't.

I don't think i've ever stopped wanting to save scum.  I eventually
learned that no matter how much I wanted it to work, no matter how much
I expected it to work, it didn't work.  No matter how obvious it was
that since it works in other games it must work in nethack, obvious does
not equal true.  But it remains frustrating that after I've finished
certain checkpoints it does not help to save a copy as a recover point. 
It took me a long time to learn.

> When you die, you learn. When you evade death, you do not learn.

"It's worse than that.  He's dead, Jim".  It worse than the fact that
you stop learning if you can replay.  The really bad thing that happens
is you make a mistake that's fatal, but it's only fatal many levels
later.  At some point you end up in a situation that's impossible to get
out of no matter how many times you try.  Because the problem is
strategy and something you did several levels early, but recovering only
works at the levels you saved.

Strategy is playing the game several levels ago though several levels
into the future. Tactics is playing the game on this level. Sorta.


